\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|    |    |RWF1|KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, N: b1-d2, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, R: f1-e1, R: f1-d1, N: f3-d2, N: f3-d4, N: f3-e1, N: f3-e5, N: f3-g5, N: f3-h4, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as White, and the position is in the early middlegame. My king has already moved (KWF1), so castling is no longer possible. Black has also moved their king (KBF1), and several pieces are developed. Notably, Black has a knight on f6 (NBF1) and a strong bishop on g7 (BBF1), both active. My light-squared bishop on c1 is still blocked in and can develop to d2, e3, f4, g5, or h6. My knights have limited options—Nb1 can go to a3, c3, or d2, while Nf3 can jump to d2, d4, e1, e5, g5, or h4. There’s a pawn on b2 that can advance two squares, and the a2 and g2, h2 pawns can also push.\n\nBlack has weakened the dark squares around their king, particularly e6, f7, and g6. My light-squared bishop could become very powerful if developed actively. Also, the center is mostly open, offering opportunities for quick development and control.\n\nEvaluation:\n- **P: a2-a4 / a2-a3**: Slow, not urgent. Doesn’t contribute much to center or development.\n- **P: b2-b4 / b2-b3**: b4 may aim for queenside expansion, but risks weakening c4 and opening lines prematurely.\n- **B: c1-g5**: A sharp developing move, pinning the knight on f6 if it were still on g8—but it's 