ould it be?\n\nPossible moves: Qd8-d6, Qd8-h4, etc. But no evidence.\n\nGiven that black hasn’t castled and king is exposed, it\'s plausible queen is still on d8.\n\nBut **riskier** than capturing undefended bishop on c5.\n\nMoreover, **N: e4-c5** is lower risk, high reward — and develops control.\n\nAlso, after Nxc5, if black recaptures with b6 or Qd8, I gain tempo.\n\nAdditionally, my knight on f7 can be vulnerable — attacked by h7 or g7 pawn soon.\n\nSo best plan: **capture the loose bishop on c5**.\n\nOther moves like pawn pushes are slower.\n\nAlso, **N: e4-d6** — forks king and rook? d6 has a black knight — so Nxe4-d6 would capture knight.\n\nBut black plays ...Nxd6, and I lose mine — equal trade.\n\nWorse than winning a bishop.\n\nHence, **N: e4-c5** is best.\n\nEvaluation:\n- N: e4-c5 — wins a bishop, low risk, central control.\n- N: f7-d8 — speculative, might win queen, but risky if queen moved.\n- P: f2-f4 — supports e4 knight, but slow.\n- N: e4-d2 — retreats, poor.\n\nReason: The black bishop on c5 is undefended, and capturing it with the knight on e4 gains a full piece (3 points) for free, significantly increasing material advantage. This move is both safe and strategically sound, reinforcing central dominance.\n\nAnswer: N: e4-c5'}
