es knight on h6\n- Bishop on h6 — less central, more passive\n- Might be trapped\n\nSo **Bxf6** is better — more central, active.\n\nAlso, f6 is closer to the black king — potential long-term pressure.\n\nMoreover, after Bxf6, if Black plays ...Kd5-e6 or something, I can have pressure.\n\nBut most importantly: **it wins a free piece**.\n\nNow, are there any checks or tactics?\n\nWhat about **N: e5-d7+**? Nd7 checks the black king? From e5 to d7 — that’s two up, one left — yes, d7 is empty. But does it attack d5? d7 to d5 is two down — not a knight move. So no.\n\nNd7 attacks c5, e5, f6, f8, c8 — not d5. So no check.\n\nBut after Bxf6, I win a knight — worth ~3 points.\n\nAlternatively, developing with pawns — like c2-c4 — but that’s slower.\n\nOr N: e5-c4 — developing, but not urgent.\n\nGiven that I can **win a free knight with B: g7-f6**, this seems like the best move.\n\nIs there any risk?\n\nCould Black recapture with the king? Kd5xe5? Only if I were on e5 — but I’m playing Bg7xf6.\n\nAfter Bxf6, Black has no piece that can capture on f6 except possibly the king — but Kd5 to f6? That’s two files over and one rank — not a legal king move. Minimum distance is √5 — not orthogonal or diagonal. So no.\n\nThus, the knight on f6 is unprotected, and I can take it with impunity.\n\nTherefore, **B: g7-f6** is a free knight.\n\nWhy not B: g7-h6? Same thing — but h6 is less valuable, and the bishop ends up on the edge.\n\nf6 is more central.\n\nHence, **B: g7-f6** is superior.\n\nEvaluation:\n- B: g7-f6 — wins a knight, active placement, no risk\n- B: g7-h6 — wins a knight, but bishop less active\n- N: e5-c4 — solid development, but slow\n- P: c2-c4 — gains space, but ignores material gain\n- Other moves ignore a free piece\n\nReason: Capturing the undefended knight on f6 with the bishop wins free material and increases my advantage. The move is safe and improves piece activity.\n\nAnswer: B: g7-f6'}
