op candidate: **P: b2-b3** — this supports c4 directly, prepares Bb2 development, and is a solid, useful move that prevents immediate loss of the c4 pawn if Black plays ...Nc6 next.\n\nAlternatively, **P: a2-a4** is also good—prevents ...b5, controls space, but does not protect c4.\n\nBut protecting c4 is more urgent than space.\n\nAnother idea: **P: c4-c5** — pushes the pawn, gains space, but makes it isolated after ...b7-b6 and ...Ba7 or ...Bxc5. Also, if I push c5, I lose the chance to support it with b3 unless b3 is played first.\n\nSo pushing c4 now is risky.\n\nTherefore, **P: b2-b3** is the most prudent—it safeguards my center, enables development, and is flexible.\n\nReason: The pawn on c4 is currently undefended and under threat from the black knight on b8, which can go to c6. Playing b2-b3 defends c4, prepares light-squared bishop development to b2, and strengthens the center without overextending.\n\nAnswer: P: b2-b3"}
