d says: f4: empty. Wait:\n\nRow 4: c4: PWF1, d4: PBF1, g4: BBF1, f4: ? — between e and g — so f4 is empty.\n\nWait, row 4:  \na4: empty, b4: empty, c4: PWF1, d4: PBF1, e4: empty, f4: empty, g4: BBF1, h4: PBF1\n\nSo f4 is empty.\n\nSo king on g5 can go to:\n- f5: empty\n- f6: empty\n- h6: empty\n- h5: empty\n- f4: empty\n- h4: empty\n- g6: attacked by rook\n- g4: empty? g4 has BBF1 — yes, black bishop — so occupied by own piece.\n\nSo king cannot go to g4.\n\nSo escape squares: f4, f5, f6, h5, h6.\n\nAfter R: h6-g6+, king must move to one of these.\n\nSuppose K:g5-f5. Then I can play R:g6-g7, threatening mate on f7 or h7, or even prepare Q-side action.\n\nAlternatively, if king goes to h5, Rh6-h8#? No — rook is on g6.\n\nBut I could chase.\n\nBut better: **R: h6-g6+** forces king away, gains tempo, and may win material.\n\nCompare to other aggressive options:\n\n- **P: f2-f4+** — pawn to f4, checks the king!\n\nf2-f4: pawn moves two squares, from f2 to f4.\n\nDoes it attack g5? Pawn on f4 attacks g5 diagonally — yes! Because white pawn moves forward, captures diagonally — so from f4, it attacks g5.\n\nAnd g5 has black king — so **P: f2-f4+** is also a **check**!\n\nAnd it\'s in the valid moves list.\n\nSo two checks available:\n1. R: h6-g6+\n2. P: f2-f4+\n\nWhich is better?\n\nEvaluate **P: f2-f4+**:\n- Delivers check to king on g5.\n- King must move to f5, f6, h6, h5, f4, h4 — same as before.\n- But after f2-f4, the pawn is now on f4, controlling g5 and e5.\n- However, the pawn may be captured — black has a bishop on g4 (BBF1), which can take on f4 en passant? No — white pawn just moved two squares, so **en passant is possible**.\n\nBlack can reply ...B:g4xf3 en passant? Wait — en passant rule: only a pawn can capture en passant.\n\nHere, Black has no pawn on e5 or g5 — g5 has king. So no black pawn adjacent to f4 on the 5th rank.\n\nTherefore, **no en passant capture possible** — because en passant is only for pawns.\n\nBlack has:\n- Pawns on b7, a5, d4, h4 — none 