y the knight — but after capture, the king is gone — wait no: when White plays Ne5, it\'s a capture — so the move is **Nc6xe5**, removing the Black king.\n\nBut in chess, the game ends when a player is in check and cannot get out — but here, if the king is on e5 and a knight moves to e5, that’s a capture — which should only be allowed if the king is not defended.\n\nBut if the king is **not in check before the move**, then moving a piece to its square would be capturing it — but that would mean the king was left en prise — which is only possible if it were stalemate or illegal position.\n\nBut in this case, **before White\'s move**, is the Black king on e5 in check?\n\nCurrently, before any move, is e5 attacked by White?\n\n- Knight on c6: c6 to e5 — yes, it\'s a knight move. So **the Black king is already under attack by the White knight on c6**.\n\nThat means **Black is already in check** — but it\'s **White\'s turn**, which is impossible — because a player cannot leave their king in check.\n\nTherefore, **this position is invalid** — unless the knight on c6 does **not** attack e5.\n\nWait: c6 to e5 — difference: +2 files (c→e), -1 rank (6→5) — yes, that\'s a knight move. So **the knight on c6 attacks e5**.\n\nAnd e5 has the Black king — so **Black is in check**.\n\nBut it is **White\'s turn to move** — meaning Black just moved into check, which is illegal.\n\nContradiction.\n\nUnless... did I misread the position?\n\nLet me double-check the board:\n\nRow 6: c6 has NWF1 — White knight on c6\n\nRow 5: e5 has KBF1 — Black king on e5\n\nDistance: c6 to e5 — c→d→e (2 files), 6→5 (1 rank down) — yes, knight can attack.\n\nSo **White knight on c6 attacks Black king on e5**.\n\nTherefore, **Black is in check**.\n\nBut now it\'s **White\'s turn** — which means Black must have made a move that left their king in check — impossible in legal chess.\n\nHence, the only logical conclusion is that **the knight on c6 does NOT attack e5 because it\'s pinned or blocked** — but ther