\n\ne5 is empty.\n\nf5 has BBF1 (Black bishop).\n\nSo Black king is on e6.\n\nNow, can the White knight on c6 attack it?\n\nc6 to e6: Δfile = +2, Δrank = 0 — that\'s two files right, same rank — not a knight move. Knight needs 2 and 1.\n\nSo c6 to e6 is not a valid knight move.\n\nClosest knight moves from c6:\n- a5, a7, b4, b8, d4, d8, e5, e7\n\ne5 and e7 are knight moves.\n\ne6 is not reachable.\n\nSo knight cannot attack king directly.\n\nNow, **Nc6-e5**: knight to e5 — is that useful?\n\ne5 is empty — moving there controls key central squares, threatens f7, d7, c6, c4, etc.\n\nBut also, from e5, the knight eyes f7 — which is currently occupied?\n\nf7: row 7: |    |PBF0|    |    |    |    |    |    | → so b7 has PBF0, f7 is empty.\n\ng7, h7: empty.\n\nf6: empty.\n\nNo obvious target.\n\nBut e5 is a strong outpost.\n\nOther moves:\n\nRook on a1 can move along first rank — all squares from b1 to g1 are empty.\n\nh1 has KWF1 — so rook can go to g1.\n\nBut not very aggressive.\n\nPawn a2-a4: gains space, prepares queenside play.\n\nBut slow.\n\nc5-d6: pawn on c5 (PWF1) to d6 — d6 has a Black knight (NBF1).\n\nSo c5xd6 — capture.\n\nAfter c5xd6, Black can recapture with e6xd6 — but then the Black king on e6 captures back — exposing itself.\n\nBut then White has a pawn on d6, advanced.\n\nBut not immediately decisive.\n\nNow, knight on c6: can go to e5.\n\nFrom e5, it attacks:\n- d7, f7, c6, c4, d3, f3, g4, g6\n\nNotably, g6 and f7 — but no pieces.\n\nBut also, **e5 attacks d7** — but d7 is empty.\n\nMore importantly, **e5 is central and hard to dislodge**.\n\nAnother knight move: c6-e7 — e7 is empty — but then knight is on e7, which is closer to the Black king on e6.\n\nFrom e7, knight attacks:\n- d5, f5, c6, c8, d9 (invalid), f9 (invalid), g8, g6\n\nSo f5 (Black bishop), d5, g6, c6.\n\nf5 has Black bishop — so Ne7 could threaten Bf5.\n\nBut Black can move the bishop.\n\nBut also, **Ne7 attacks d5 and f5**, and is near e6.\n\nBut knight on e7 is one square away from e