 in row 5: BBF1 is on f5? Let\'s recheck.\n\nWait — correction: On f5 there is BBF1? Looking back:\n\nRow 5: |PBF1|    |NWF1|    |    |BBF1|    |    | → So yes, Black bishop on f5 (BBF1). That means e6 is defended by the bishop on f5? Diagonally: f5 to e6 is one diagonal step — yes, e6 is under control by BBF1. So N: c5-e6 would be captured by BBF1.\n\nSo N: c5-e6 is unsafe.\n\nSimilarly, N: c5-d7 attacks nothing and walks into danger.\n\nN: c5-b7 – captures a pawn! b7 has PBF0. Is that legal?\n\nc5 to b7: Knight move — c5 to b7 is two up, one left? No: c to b is one left, 5 to 7 is two up — that’s an L-shape: yes, valid. And b7 has PBF0 (Black pawn, never moved). So N: c5-b7 is a capture!\n\nThat wins material — a free pawn. Is it safe?\n\nAfter N: c5-b7, Black can recapture with a8 rook? But a8 is empty. Who is on a8? Board shows a8 empty. b8? Empty. c8? Empty. d8? Nothing. So Black has no piece near b7. Only potential recapture is from a6 or c6 — but both are empty. So N: c5-b7 captures a pawn and cannot be immediately recaptured. Huge material gain!\n\nBut wait — is b7 occupied only by PBF0? Yes. And no adjacent Black pieces. So this is a free pawn grab.\n\nWhy didn\'t I see this earlier?\n\nAlso, after N: c5-b7, the knight is on b7, which is deep in enemy territory, but safe for now.\n\nThis seems like a very strong move.\n\nOther moves like P: g2-g4 or rook moves are positional, but this is a tactical win.\n\nNow, is N: c5-b7 in the valid moves list?\n\nValid moves: "N: c5-a4, N: c5-a6, N: c5-b7, N: c5-d3, N: c5-d7, N: c5-e4, N: c5-e6"\n\nYes! N: c5-b7 is listed.\n\nTherefore, capturing the undefended b7 pawn with the knight is a winning move.\n\nReason: Capturing on b7 wins a free pawn with no risk of immediate recapture, significantly increasing material advantage. It also disrupts Black’s queenside structure and activates the knight aggressively. This move maximizes winning probability compared to slow development or pawn pushes.\n\nAnswer: N: c5-b7'}
