ntly empty? Wait — no: g1 is empty, but h1 is my king. So Qa7-g1 is possible only if the path is clear.\n\nWait — Qa7-g1: path goes through a7-b7-c7-d7-e7-f7-g7, then down to g1? No — that’s vertical? No! From a7 to g1 is diagonal: a7→b6→c5→d4→e3→f2→g1 — yes! That’s a valid diagonal. And all squares are empty. So **Qa7-g1** is a diagonal move ending at g1, which would attack along the long diagonal.\n\nBut g1 is currently unoccupied. And the black king is on d3 — so Qg1 would control g1-a7 diagonal, but does it check? Only if the black king is on that diagonal — d3 is not on a7-g1 diagonal. So no check.\n\nBut more importantly: **Qa7-f2** — this hits f2, and from there, it eyes g1 and h3. But again, not immediately decisive.\n\nWait — what about **Nf3-e1**? That blocks my rook on a1? Not useful.\n\nBetter idea: **Nf3-e5** — central outpost, eyes f7, d7, c6, f3, etc., but black king is on d3 — not directly threatened.\n\nEven better: **Qa7-e3+**! Because e3 is on the same rank as d3 — wait, no: e3 is one square up from d3? d3 and e3 are adjacent horizontally.\n\nYes! **Qa7-e3** attacks e3, which is adjacent to d3 — so if the queen moves to e3, it checks the black king!\n\nIs that true? Black king is on d3. Queen on e3 would be one square to the right — same rank — so yes, horizontal attack → check.\n\nAnd is the path clear? a7→b6→c5→d4→e3 — all these squares are empty? Let\'s verify:\n\n- b6: empty\n- c5: empty\n- d4: empty\n- e3: empty\n\nYes! So **Q: a7-e3** is a legal move and delivers check.\n\nNow, can the black king escape? From d3, it can go to:\n\n- c2 (but c2 is attacked by my pawn on b3? No — b3 is my pawn (PWF1), which attacks a4 and c4 — not c2.\n- c4? But c4 is not adjacent — king moves one square.\n\nKing on d3 can go to:\n\n- c3, c4, d4, e4, e3, e2, d2, c2 — but many of these may be attacked.\n\nBut after Qe3+, the king must move. Possible escapes:\n\n- c2: possibly safe?\n- c3: maybe\n- e2: e2 is attacked by my knight on f3? Nf3 attacks e1, g1, d4, d2, h4, e5, g5 — not e2. So e2 might be safe?\n\nBut regardless, **delivering check forces Black to respond**, and likely lose tempo or blunder.\n\nAlternatively, **Nf3-e1** is passive.\n\nOr **Pb3-a4**: captures nothing, weakens structure.\n\nOr **Rook moves** — but rook on a1 moving to b1 or c1 does little.\n\nBest is **Qa7-e3+**, delivering check and increasing pressure on the exposed black king.\n\nAlso, **Qa7-g1** is possible, but doesn\'t check — just positions the queen.\n\nBut **Qa7-f2**? f2 is on the same diagonal as g1 and a7, but again, not checking.\n\nOnly **Qe3** gives check.\n\nAnother candidate: **Nf3-d4** — attacks d4, which is adjacent to d3 — so Nd4+ also checks the black king!\n\nNd4+: knight from f3 to d4 — L-shape: 2 left (f→d), 1 up (3→4)? No: f to d is 2 files left, 3 to 4 is 1 rank up — yes, "L" shape — valid.\n\nd4 is adjacent to d3 — so knight on d4 attacks d3 → check.\n\nSo both **Qa7-e3** and **Nf3-d4** deliver check.\n\nWhich is better?\n\n- Qe3+ attacks along the file — queen comes in with maximum threat. After Qe3+, if Black responds, say, Kd3-c2, then I can play Qe3-c1+ or follow up with rook activation.\n\n- Nd4+ — knight checks, but knight can be captured by king if any piece defends? King can take if it wants — but d3 to d4? No, king cannot capture its own knight? Wait — no black knight on d4.\n\nAfter Nd4+, black king can:\n\n- Capture the knight: Kd3xd4 — then I recapture? Do I have anything attacking d4? Queen on a7 can go to d4? a7→d4 is diagonal — a7-b6-c5-d4 — yes! So if Kxd4, then Qa7-d4 recaptures, winning back material and maintaining strong position.\n\nBut wait — after Nd4+, if Kxd4, then Qxd4 — now my queen controls the center and threatens multiple things.\n\nBut better yet: if Nd4+, and K moves away (say Kc2), then I still have queen coming in.\n\nBut **queen check is stronger** because:\n\n- Queen is more valuable — but here, we\'re giving check and not losing it.\n- Queen on e3 controls more squares: entire e-file, 3rd rank, and diagonals.\n- It also eyes g1 for potential mating nets.\n\nMoreover, **Qe3+** prevents Black from consolidating and may lead to faster win.\n\nAlso, **Qa7-e3** is in the valid move list: “Q: a7-e3” — yes, listed.\n\nSimilarly, “N: f3-d4” is listed.\n\nBut Qe3+ is more forcing and powerful.\n\nAlso, consider: after Qe3+, if Kc2, then Ra1-c1+ forks king and possibly other pieces? Rc1+ followed by Rxc1+ Kxc1, then Qxe3 — but no, queen is already on e3.\n\nWait — if Qe3+ Kc2, then Rc1+ Kd2, then Qe3-e2#? Queen to e2 checkmates? e2 — is that possible?\n\nFrom e3 to e2 — down one square — yes.\n\nIf king is on d2, then Qe2# — because e2 controls e2, and king on d2 has no escape — surrounded.\n\nBut let’s see: after Qe3+ Kc2, then Ra1-c1+ Kd2, then Qe3-e2# — yes! Checkmate.\n\nIs that forced?\n\nBlack king on d3.\n\n1. Qa7-e3+ Kd3-c2 (only escape? What about Kd3-e2?)\n\nKd3-e2: is that possible?\n\ne2 — is it attacked?\n\nCurrently: my knight on f3 attacks d2 and d4, not e2.\n\nMy pawn on b3 attacks a4 and c4.\n\nMy pawn on g2 attacks h3 and f3.\n\nNo piece attacks e2 directly.\n\nSo Ke2 is possible.\n\nAfter Qe3+ Ke2, then what?\n\nThen Qe3-e2# — same thing! Queen takes on e2 with checkmate.\n\nBecause king on e2, queen on e2 — but no: you can’t capture king, but queen moving to e2 gives check — and king on e2 cannot move — all surrounding squares:\n\n- d1: attacked by queen?\n- d2: not necessarily controlled\n- d3: free?\n- e1: ?\n- e3: queen came from there\n- f1: ?\n- f2: ?\n- f3: ?\n\nActually, king on e2, queen on e2 — impossible. The queen moves to e2, attacking e2 — so if king is on e2, and queen moves to e2, that would be capturing, but in chess, you don’t capture the king — you checkmate.\n\nBut the rule is: if the king is in check and cannot escape, it\'s checkmate.\n\nSo after Qe3+ Ke2, then Qe3-e2 is not legal because the queen cannot move to a square occupied by the king? No — when giving check, the queen moves to e2, which is where the king is — but in standard chess notation, you can\'t move to a square occupied by your own piece — but here, the king is on e2, so the queen cannot move to e2.\n\nAh! Critical mistake.\n\nThe queen cannot capture the king — but in practice, when a piece gives check by moving to a square adjacent or attacking, it doesn\'t need to occupy the king\'s square.\n\nBut in this case, **Qe3-e2** is moving to e2 — which is where the king is — so it\'s an illegal move.\n\nYou cannot move a piece to a square occupied by your own king.\n\nSo after Qe3+ Ke2, I cannot play Qe2.\n\nBut I can play other moves.\n\nFor example, after Qe3+ Ke2, then Nf3-d4+? But d4 is not attacking e2.\n\nKnight on f3 attacks d2, d4, e1, e5, g1, g5, h4 — not e2.\n\nSo no knight check.\n\nRa1-e1+? That would be possible — rook to e1, checking.\n\nBut is it in the move list? Valid moves include: R: a1-b1, a1-c1, ..., a1-e1 — yes! “R: a1-e1” is listed.\n\nSo after Qe3+ Ke2, then Re1+! Then king must go to d2 or f2.\n\nSay Kd2, then Qe3-e2#? Now e2 is empty — yes! King on d2, queen to e2 — controls e2, d2, d3, e3, f2, f3 — king on d2 has:\n\n- c1, c2, c3, d1, d3, e1, e2, e3\n\nQueen on e2 controls e2, d2 (diagonally?), no — queen on e2 controls:\n\n- Entire e-file\n- Entire 2nd rank\n- Diagonals: d1, c2, b3, a4; and f1, g2, h3; and d3, c4, b5, a6; and f3, g4, h5\n\nSo from e2, queen attacks:\n\n- d1, d2, d3\n- e1, e3\n- f1, f2, f3\n- c2, c4, etc.\n\nSo yes — king on d2 is attacked by queen on e2.\n\nCan it move? To c1? Is c1 attacked? By queen? c1 is not on e-file, not on 2nd rank, not diagonal (e2→d1→c0 — no). So c1 is not attacked.\n\nSo after Re1+ Kd2, then Qe3-e2+? But king can escape to c1.\n\nThen no mate.\n\nBut I can play Qe3-d3+? d3 is behind — but d3 is not attacked by queen from e3? Queen on e3 attacks d3? Yes — horizontally left.\n\nSo Qe3-d3+? But d3 is where the king was — but now it\'s on e2.\n\nAfter Qe3+ Ke2, I can play Qe3-d3? That’s retreating — not helpful.\n\nBetter: after Qe3+ Ke2, play Nf3-e1+! Knight to e1 — is that a check?\n\nNf3-e1: f3 to e1 — 1 left, 2 down — yes, L-shape.\n\ne1 is adjacent to e2 — knight on e1 attacks d3, f3, c2, g2 — not e2.\n\nSo no.\n\nSo no immediate mate.\n\nBut still, **Qe3+** forces the black king into a worse position and loses tempo.\n\nBack to Nd4+.\n\nNf3-d4+ Kd3xd4 — then Qa7-d4 — now queen controls d4, and threatens mate on d3 or c3.\n\nBut better: after Kxd4, Qd4+ Kb5? Or something.\n\nBut perhaps Qe3+ is simpler and more direct.\n\nAnother idea: **Qa7-e7** — no, not in the direction.\n\nWait — best is **Qa7-e3+** — it\'s a check, it\'s forcing, and it activates the most powerful piece.\n\nIt\'s superior to knight check because the queen is more dangerous and harder to deal with.\n\nAlso, after Qe3+, if Kc2, then Ra1-c1+! As I thought earlier — Rc1+ Kd2, then Qe3-e2# — but again, e2 is not attacked by queen from e3 if king is on d2? No.\n\nAfter Qe3+ Kc2, then Rc1+ Kd2, now can I play Qe3-e2+? Yes — queen from e3 to e2 — e2 is empty.\n\nNow, queen on e2 attacks:\n\n- Entire e-file: e1, e2, e3, e4...\n- 2nd rank: a2 to h2\n- Diagonals: d1, c2, b3, a4; f1, g2, h3; d3, c4, b5, a6; f3, g4, h5\n\nSo from e2, queen attacks d3 and f3 — so king on d2 is attacked on d2? Is d2 on the same diagonal?\n\ne2 to d2 is one square left — horizontal — yes! Same rank? No — e2 and d2 are adjacent files, same rank? No — d2 and e2 are both on rank 2.\n\nYes! d2 and e2 are on the same rank — rank 2.\n\nSo queen on e2 controls the entire 2nd rank — including d2.\n\nSo yes — Qe2+ checks the king on d2.\n\nNow, can the king escape?\n\n- c2: attacked by queen? c2 is on 2nd rank — yes, queen controls c2.\n- c1: not on 2nd rank — c1 is rank 1. Is it attacked? By queen? Queen on e2 — diagonal to c2 (e2→d1→c0? No). e2 to c2 is two left, zero up — horizontal? e2 to c2 is two files left — yes, same rank — so yes, c2 is attacked.\n\nBut c1? File c, rank 1.\n\nNot on same file, not same rank, not diagonal: e2→d1→c0 — no. e2 to c1: difference of 2 files, 1 rank — not diagonal.\n\nDiagonals from e2: to d1 (1 left, 1 down), c0 invalid; to f1 (1 right, 1 down), g0 invalid.\n\nSo c1 is not attacked.\n\nSo king can go to c1.\n\nThen no mate.\n\nBut I can follow with Qe2-c2+ or something.\n\nStill, the point is: **Qe3+** is a strong, forcing move that disrupts Black and gains initiative.\n\nMoreover, it\'s in the valid move list.\n\nOther moves like P: g2-g4 or N: f3-h4 are slower.\n\nTherefore, best choice is **Q: a7-e3**\n\nAnswer: Q: a7-e3'}
